= Introduction
In the modern times, medicine has become a scientific field with vast improvement and many complex procedures due to improves methods and technological help. Alongside that, medical procedures became more standardised with best practices and common ways to treat illnesses and medical issues. But the sheer complexity of medical knowledge and procedures led to an increased difficulty for patients to understand these procedures and grasp their effect on their health and wellbeeing.

In the German healthcare system, medical doctors are required by law in § 630d BGB to do informed consent consultations for their patients prior to a surgerey or medical procedure @bgb_630d. That paragraph dictates that patient need to consent into their upcoming medical procedure, while § 630e BGB governs the actual prerequisites that are need to be met for given consent to be valid, like information on reasons for the procedure, alternatives, possible side effects and description of the procedure itself @bgb_630e.

While these laws govern the informed consent, the form and amount is not specified exactly. It can be done only in spoken form during the consultation or can optionally be facilitated by leaflets or digital media.

== Problem
Even though medical informed consent leaflet exists, these text are often difficult to understand as well, which makes them not ideal to patients to read without any possibility to ask questions or request clarification. Medical doctors also don't have a high amount of time for their patients which results in rather short and superficial consultations that do not result in a good understanding of the planned procedure for the patients.

== Research questions
The main research question for this thesis is:

_How can a digital product prototype for LLM-based informed patient content be designed and implemented?_

This research question falls easily into two main parts that each deal with one aspect of the research question. The first part focusses mainly on the applied and technical side of things, dealing more with design regards, software development and DevOps.

The second subquestion builds on top of the results of the first subquestion. It is closer to scientific processes and utilises methods from NLP and generative AI to evaluate quality of the system build in the first part.

== Relation to Digital Humanities
Digital Humanities is the traditionally the application of methods from computer science in the humanities. This project is on surface level closer to computer science or medical informatics, but on closer inspection it falls into the area of interaction between society and technology.

== Thieme Compliance
Thieme Compliance is part of the Thieme Group and specialised on medical informed consent. It is the result of the aquisition of the two companies Diomed and proCompliance which were merged to the company Thieme Compliance. The main product of Thieme Compliance are informed consent leaflets that cover more than 2000 medical procedures in more than 20 languages. Those leaflets can either be ordered as paper leaflets or used in digital form via the companies own software called eConsentPro. The digital workflow includes several advantages over the paper based flow through digital tools like pre-filling fields with already know patient data or pre-filtering amanesis questions that are relevant for the patient at hand like skipping questions about pregnancy on male patients.

== Thesis Structure
This thesis is structured into two main parts where the first part focusses on the development of the prototype app along with design decisions and implementation against business demands and constraints. The second part is an evaluation of the system that was designed and implemented in the first part. Focus here is to assess the quality using different metrics, to see how well the prototype achieves quality requirements.
